>> A1: Copy here the declaration of each new or changed `struct' or
>> `struct' member, global or static variable, `typedef', or
>> enumeration.  Identify the purpose of each in 25 words or less.

struct sleep_entry {
  struct thread *thread;      
  int64_t wakeup_time;   
  struct semaphore sema;  
  struct list_elem elem;  
};
static struct list sleep_list;
static struct lock sleep_list_lock;

******
The purpose of the sleep_entry struct is to store information about a thread.
The sleep_list data structure is a list containing sleep_entry structs that stores information about all the threads that are sleeping.
The sleep_list_lock is a lock used to protect the sleep_list data structure while it is being modified.
******

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.

When timer_sleep() is called, the current thread is suspended (or sleeps) for a given number of ticks. 
The timer interrupt handler wakes up the thread when the timer expires.
All of this is accomplished by adding the current thread to the sleep_list and acquiring the sleep_list_lock.

>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler?

The timer interrupt handler is minimized by only waking up the thread when the timer expires. 
The list is ordered by wake_up time, so the thread with the earliest wake_up time gets checked on first,
allowing the other threads to continue sleeping.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: How are race conditions avoided when multiple threads call
>> timer_sleep() simultaneously?

Race conditions get avoided by acquiring the sleep_list_lock when modifying the sleep_list.
This ensures that only one thread can modify the list at a time, preventing race conditions.

>> A5: How are race conditions avoided when a timer interrupt occurs
>> during a call to timer_sleep()?

Race conditions get avoided by turning off interrupts, acquiring the sleep_list_lock, and then turning interrupts back on.
This makes sure that the timer interrupt handler won't  modify the list of sleeping threads while timer_sleep() is acquiring the lock.


---- RATIONALE ----

>> A6: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

This design was chosen because it is straightforward and employs 
simple data structures and synchronization mechanisms already available in Pintos. 
Anything more involved than this solution likely would have introduced unnecessary overhead and complexity.
Additionally, the use of an ordered list to store the sleeping threads ensures that the thread with the earliest 
wake_up time gets checked on first, speeding up the overall process. 

