---- DATA STRUCTURES ----

>> B1: Copy here the declaration of each new or changed `struct' or
>> `struct' member, global or static variable, `typedef', or
>> enumeration.  Identify the purpose of each in 25 words or less.
struct thread
  {
    int base_priority;            
    struct lock *waiting_lock;    
    struct list donations;        /* List of threads that donated priority to this thread. */
    struct list_elem donation_elem; 
    struct semaphore donation_sem;
    struct list_elem sema_elem;
  }
Base priority is for reverting back to the original priority given at thread creation after being converted to a temporary "donated priority".
Waiting_lock is the lock this thread is waiting for (if any).
Donation_elem is for an element to be put on lower-priority thread's waiting list, so that we can track all the donors.
Donation_sem is semaphore used for synchronization when adjusting or restoring donation-related priorities.
Sema_elem allows the thread to be placed in semaphore wait lists in correct priority order.
>> B2: Explain the data structure used to track priority donation.
>> Use ASCII art to diagram a nested donation.  (Alternately, submit a
>> .png file.)

            (waiting_lock)
     Thread B (priority 50)  ----------+
                                       |
                                       v
                                 [Lock L1]
                                  holder
                                       |
                                       v
                         Thread A (priority 20)  --------+
                                                        |
                                               (waiting_lock)
                                                        |
                                                        v
                                                  [Lock L2]
                                                   holder
                                                        |
                                                        v
                                            Thread C (priority 10)
 
* Thread B donates its priority to Thread A (because A holds Lock L1).
* If Thread A is also stuck waiting for Lock L2 from Thread C, A’s elevated priority can be donated further up to Thread C, ensuring C runs sooner and releases L2 so A can proceed.
* After L2 is released by thread C, thread is able to acquire L2, while thread c is reverted to its original priority (10).
* Thread A continues at the donated priority (50) while it holds Lock L2. Once it finishes its work with Lock L2, it releases it. If there are no more donors waiting on A’s locks, A’s priority reverts to 20.
* Eventually, A will release Lock L1, allowing B to acquire Lock L1. At this point, B no longer needs to donate its priority to A or C, and B itself continues at its original (or possibly updated) priority.
---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B3: How do you ensure that the highest priority thread waiting for
>> a lock, semaphore, or condition variable wakes up first?
Since all waiting threads are stored in an ordered list (or priority queue) that is sorted by each thread’s current priority,
when the lock (or semaphore, or condition) becomes available, the scheduler always removes and unblocks the front of this sorted list, which holds the thread of highest priority.
>> B4: Describe the sequence of events when a call to lock_acquire()
>> causes a priority donation.  How is nested donation handled?

When lock_acquire() is called and the lock is held by a lower-priority thread, here’s what happens:

Disable interrupts - Temporarily turn off interrupts to prevent context switches during donation.
Lock the recipient’s donation list - Use a semaphore (e.g., donation_sem) to guard the recipient’s donation list so we can safely modify it.
Give higher priority & record Ddonor - Assign the donor’s higher priority to the recipient if it’s lower, and insert the donor into the recipient’s donations list (sorted by priority) to track all donors.
Handle nested donation - if the current recipient (lock holder) is itself waiting on another lock, treat that holder as the new donor, with the next lock holder as the new recipient. Repeat until you reach a thread that isn’t blocked by another lock.
Restore interrupts - After walking through the entire nested chain, re-enable interrupts to resume normal scheduling.

>> B5: Describe the sequence of events when lock_release() is called
>> on a lock that a higher-priority thread is waiting for.
The following events happen:
Disable interrupts - Prevent context switches by disabling interrupts 
Remove relevant donor- In the releasing thread’s donations list, remove any donors that were waiting on this lock—since they are no longer blocked by it.
Refresh releaser’s priority- Revert the lock holder’s priority to its base_priority or the highest remaining donor’s priority (if it holds other locks still blocking threads).
Wake the next thread- In terms of what happen to this just-released-lock, we call sema_up to unblock the highest-priority thread waiting for the lock (it is removed from the semaphore’s sorted wait list).
Restore interrupts- Re-enable interrupts and allow the scheduler to preempt if the newly unblocked thread has a higher priority.
---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B6: Describe a potential race in thread_set_priority() and explain
>> how your implementation avoids it.  Can you use a lock to avoid
>> this race?
If a thread calls thread_set_priority() while another thread (or the scheduler) is simultaneously updating that same thread’s priority—e.g., due to priority donation—both updates might interleave in a way that corrupts the final priority or yields incorrect scheduling decisions
My implementation avoids it by disabling interrupts around scheduling operations (although thread_set_priority() itself doesn’t explicitly disable interrupts, other parts of the scheduling system (e.g., thread_yield(), thread_unblock()) do.)
Additionally, When donation updates a thread’s priority, it acquires donation_sem to protect concurrent modifications. Thus, donation and explicit thread_set_priority() calls don’t interfere with each other at the same time.
Using a lock might be risky because it would still enable context switch, and if the scheduler preempts while holding—or waiting for—the same lock, the system may deadlock.
---- RATIONALE ----

>> B7: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?

We chose this design becasue it has clear separation of responsibilities: for example, we separate a thread's original priority vs gifted priority, 
so that we can easily revert to the original value when it is not longer apply. We also felt that it is neccesary to create donated-related variable like donations, waiting_locks, and etc,
because since gifting/restoring priority happen so frequently (and often times iterative), it is important to build the link between donors-recipient, lock-lock waiters.
In addition, we added per-thread semaphore for safety since multiple donor threads could've modified recipient thread's donor list, which might caused bottlenecks or deadlocks if not protected.
Alternative implementation wise, we've considered using a global variable for the donations list, but that overlooked the fact that each thread has different donors so I think our implementation is the most straightforward and flexible one of all.

